{"name":"Pureed Pumpkin","hex":"#c74222","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pureed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of puree"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pureed"},{"term":"pumpkin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A domesticated plant, in species Cucurbita pepo, similar in growth pattern, foliage, flower, and fruit to the squash or melon.","The round yellow or orange fruit of this plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pumpkin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Purée","blocks":[{"text":"A purée is cooked food, usually vegetables, fruits or legumes, that has been ground, pressed, blended or sieved to the consistency of a creamy paste or liquid. Purées of specific foods are often known by specific names, e.g., apple sauce or hummus. The term is of French origin, where it meant in Old French purified or refined."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Cauliflower_puree.jpg/330px-Cauliflower_puree.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pur%C3%A9e"},{"title":"Pumpkin","blocks":[{"text":"A pumpkin is a cultivated winter squash in the genus Cucurbita. The term is most commonly applied to round, orange-colored squash varieties, but does not possess a scientific definition. It may be used in reference to many different squashes of varied appearance and belonging to multiple species in the Cucurbita genus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/FrenchMarketPumpkinsB.jpg/330px-FrenchMarketPumpkinsB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pumpkin"},{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Cucurbita_2011_G1_Large.jpg/500px-Cucurbita_2011_G1_Large.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Cucurbita_2011_G1_Large.jpg/1280px-Cucurbita_2011_G1_Large.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Cucurbita_2011_G1_Large.jpg/500px-Cucurbita_2011_G1_Large.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A variety of pumpkin cultivars. The central and rightmost orange fruits are Cucurbita pepo, all others are Cucurbita maxima","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACucurbita_2011_G1_Large.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Pumpkin_Field.jpg/500px-Pumpkin_Field.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Pumpkin_Field.jpg/1280px-Pumpkin_Field.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Pumpkin_Field.jpg/500px-Pumpkin_Field.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A field of giant pumpkins","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APumpkin_Field.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Cross_section_of_pumpkin.jpg